---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ExtractImagesFromPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf/ExtractImagesFromPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="extract-images-from-pdf">
  <ExtractImagesFromPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>Extracting is not the same as converting</h2>
      <p>
        Two very different operations are both called &ldquo;PDF to image&rdquo;, and the wrong one
        costs you quality.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col"></th>
            <th scope="col">Extract images (this page)</th>
            <th scope="col"><a href="/pdf-to-jpg">PDF to JPG</a></th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>What you get</td>
            <td>The photographs stored inside the file</td>
            <td>A picture of each whole page</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Resolution</td>
            <td>Exactly as embedded, often 3000 px or more</td>
            <td>The DPI you choose when rendering</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Includes text and background</td>
            <td>No</td>
            <td>Yes</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Quality loss</td>
            <td>None for JPEGs: the original bytes</td>
            <td>A new encode of a new render</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Use it for</td>
            <td>Recovering artwork, product shots, figures</td>
            <td>Previews, thumbnails, sending a page as an image</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>How the originals come out untouched</h2>
      <p>
        A PDF is an object graph. A photograph on a page is usually stored as an image XObject whose stream
        is a complete JPEG file: the same bytes the camera or the design tool made, in a dictionary that
        records its width, height, colour space and compression.
      </p>
      <p>
        This tool walks that object graph with pdf-lib and finds each image XObject. When the compression is
        <code>DCTDecode</code> (JPEG), it writes the stream directly to a file, with no decode and no
        re-encode. The download is byte-for-byte what was in the document. The gallery labels these results
        <strong>Original</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Images stored with Flate or LZW compression (usually screenshots, logos and images that started as
        PNG) must be decompressed, un-predicted, converted from their colour space and encoded again. That
        path is still lossless: the PNG output has exactly the same pixels.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What gets skipped, and why we say so</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Images below the size filter. Documents contain many 1 × 1 spacers, gradient strips and hairline
          rules stored as images. The default 32 px filter removes this noise; lower it to find a small
          icon.
        </li>
        <li>
          CCITT Group 3/4 and JBIG2: bi-level fax and document-scanner codecs. No browser can decode them,
          and a custom decoder could easily give wrong results, so the tool lists them with the reason.
        </li>
        <li>
          Unusual filter chains: an image encoded two or three times in sequence is rare, and the tool does
          not guess.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Each skipped object appears in the collapsible list with its dimensions, its page and the reason. So
        if the tool returns eleven of your fifteen images, you know why four are missing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>&ldquo;It found nothing&rdquo;</h2>
      <p>
        There are two likely causes. First, the artwork can be <strong>vector</strong>: charts, diagrams,
        logos and line drawings are usually drawing instructions, not images. The file has no picture to
        extract, and the only way to get one is to render the page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Second, the document can be a <strong>scan</strong>. Then there is exactly one image per page, and it
        is the whole page. The tool finds these, and they are often very large: a 600 DPI A4 scan is about
        5000 × 7000 pixels.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Colour spaces, masks and duplicates</h2>
      <p>
        The tool decodes indexed palettes, DeviceGray, DeviceRGB, DeviceCMYK, ICC-based and calibrated
        spaces, at 1, 2, 4, 8 or 16 bits per component. Soft masks (the alpha channel of a transparent logo,
        stored as a separate grayscale image) are decoded and applied to the PNG output, so a logo keeps its
        transparency and is not on a black square.
      </p>
      <p>
        An image used on more than one page is stored once in the PDF and extracted once here, and its tile
        lists all pages that use it. A watermark on all 200 pages gives you one file, not 200.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>A note on rights</h2>
      <p>
        If you can extract an image, that does not mean you may use it. Photographs in reports, papers and
        brochures are usually licensed, and the licence is seldom in the PDF. Use extraction to recover your
        own artwork from a file whose source is lost, to take figures from a paper you cite, or to audit
        what a document contains, not to take another person&rsquo;s photography.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
